  "statements": {
    "magnetic-flux": "The magnetic flux through an area is the field strength times the area times the cosine of the angle between the field and the perpendicular to the area, $\\Phi = BA\\cos\\theta$, and its units are $\\text{T}\\cdot\\text{m}^2$. A large loop gathers more field than a small one, and a loop turned edge-on to the field gathers none. Every induced emf in this chapter is the rate at which this one quantity changes.",
    "electric-generator": "An electric generator is a coil turned in a magnetic field, with rings and brushes to carry the induced emf away. Turning the coil changes the angle between the field and the coil's perpendicular, so the flux through the coil changes, and by Faraday's law an emf appears. The generator is the same machine as the motor of the preceding chapter, with the shaft turned by hand instead of by a current."
  },
  "new": [
